/* Each event-type option carries a checkbox drawn from check_radio_sheet.png, a sprite
   whose tick is baked at #333 against a TRANSPARENT interior â€” there is no glyph or text
   node here, so the tick cannot be recoloured with CSS. In light mode that transparency
   shows the white panel through, so the dark tick reads; on the now-dark panel it is
   black-on-black and the option looks unselected even when it is checked. Repainting just
   the interior light restores exactly the light-mode rendering, so no dark-variant asset
   is needed. (filter: invert() was rejected: it would also flip the #bcbcbc outline dark
   and hide the empty box instead.) DIS-3624. */
[data-theme="dark"] #react-upcoming-events-widget-root .rw-list-option .styled-input.ui-element:where(:not(.page-builder-content *, .rsvp-ticket-modal *)) {
  background-color: var(--theme-palette-dark);
  /* Mirrors the twin checkbox repaint in new-dashboard.less:1211, which uses the
     --dashboard-theme-radius-xs token. That token is defined only in the dashboard
     :root (new-dashboard.css / client_dashboard.html), which this public events page
     never loads â€” so the 3px fallback is what actually renders here. Referencing the
     token keeps the two repaints textually in sync without depending on it resolving. */
  border-radius: var(--dashboard-theme-radius-xs, 3px);
}
/* City/region search (react-geosuggest) renders its own in-tree <ul> â€” theme.css
   previously styled only the input, so the suggestion panel kept its light LESS hex. */
[data-theme="dark"] #react-upcoming-events-widget-root .geosuggest__suggests:where(:not(.page-builder-content *, .rsvp-ticket-modal *)) {
  background-color: var(--theme-palette-light) !important;
  border-color: var(--theme-palette-light-borders) !important;
}
/* Same guard as .rw-list-option above, for the same reason: an unguarded
   `transparent !important` at ID specificity (1,2,0) out-!importants the geosuggest
   hover/active rule (generalstyles.css ~:1480, (0,4,1)) and paints the hovered /
   keyboard-active suggestion the same colour as the panel behind it â€” erasing the
   cue. Exclude .geosuggest__item--active too: it's a sibling class, so a bare
   :not(:hover) wouldn't cover keyboard navigation. Pin the text colour as well (as
   .rw-list-option does) so a tenant that stored a literal in
   GENERAL_SEARCH_DROPDOWN_ITEM_FONT_COLOR doesn't get dark-on-dark on the darkened
   panel. DIS-3624. */
[data-theme="dark"] #react-upcoming-events-widget-root
  .geosuggest__item:not(:hover):not(.geosuggest__item--active):where(:not(.page-builder-content *, .rsvp-ticket-modal *)) {
  background-color: transparent !important;
  color: var(--theme-palette-dark) !important;
}
/* The "powered by Google" attribution badge (global.less ~:1096) uses
   powered_by_google_on_white.png â€” a 144x18 sprite that is 66% TRANSPARENT with dark
   grey "powered by" lettering baked in as pixels (there is no text node here, so the
   lettering cannot be restyled with CSS). On a dark panel that grey lettering
   disappears into the background and only the coloured Google logo survives, which
   fails Google's attribution requirement.

   Swap to the non-white variant, which carries the same logo with WHITE lettering.
   Only the image changes â€” position/size still come from the global.less rule.

   Scoped to the two geosuggest panels this PR actually darkens, by mount ID, rather
   than root-scoped on [data-theme="dark"]. The white-lettering sprite is correct ONLY
   where the panel behind it is dark:
   - #react-upcoming-events-widget-root â€” the events-page panel, painted
     --theme-palette-light by the .geosuggest__suggests rule above.
   - #react-location-widget-root â€” the homepage / section location widget, whose
     LocationWidget/styles.less li background is var(--theme-palette-light), which flips
     dark. The bare id is the stable hook: .location-widget is only an optional
     extra_class (custom_mezzanine_upcoming_events_w_filters.html), and the ebay/uipath
     mounts (clients/{ebay,uipath}/src/js/*.js) query the id with no class.
   A bare [data-theme="dark"] selector would also reach a THIRD geosuggest panel â€”
   ReadMoreSidebar (#react-read-more-sidebar-root, index.html gated by
   SHOW_READ_MORE_SIDEBAR, on for startupgrind) â€” whose rows are a hardcoded white
   `li { background: #fff }` (read_more_sidebar.less:44) that this PR never darkens.
   White lettering on that still-white panel would erase the attribution there, moving
   the exact bug this rule fixes rather than eliminating it, so it is excluded. (C-H1)

   Rejected: `filter: invert()` would recolour the Google logo itself (brand
   violation); painting a white chip behind the sprite reads as a sticker on the dark
   panel and clips the logo. Lives here rather than in global.less because that file's
   pre-existing `media-query-no-invalid` violations would be surfaced (and
   destructively auto-"fixed") by the changed-files stylelint run. DIS-3624. */
[data-theme="dark"] #react-upcoming-events-widget-root
  .geosuggest__suggests-wrapper
  .geosuggest__suggests
  li:last-child:where(:not(.page-builder-content *, .rsvp-ticket-modal *))::after,
[data-theme="dark"] #react-location-widget-root
  .geosuggest__suggests-wrapper
  .geosuggest__suggests
  li:last-child:where(:not(.page-builder-content *, .rsvp-ticket-modal *))::after {
  background-image: url("/static/images/powered_by_google_on_non_white.png");
}

/* swal2 error/confirmation modals (library default white panel) â€” app-wide, not
   scoped to the events page. The brand OK button (.swal2-confirm) stays branded. */
[data-theme="dark"] .swal2-modal:where(:not(.page-builder-content *, .rsvp-ticket-modal *)) {
  background-color: var(--theme-palette-light) !important;
}
[data-theme="dark"] .swal2-modal .swal2-title:where(:not(.page-builder-content *, .rsvp-ticket-modal *)),
[data-theme="dark"] .swal2-modal .swal2-content:where(:not(.page-builder-content *, .rsvp-ticket-modal *)) {
  color: var(--theme-palette-dark) !important;
}

/* The swal2 success icon's masks â€” the same defect the `.sweet-alert` block below fixes
   for sweetalert v1, in the library the block above already darkens. swal2 draws the green
   check as a circle with three PANEL-COLOURED shapes painted over the parts that must not
   show yet: two rotated wedges (`swal2-success-circular-line-left`/`-right`) and a bar
   (`swal2-success-fix`). They are camouflage, so they must resolve to the SAME token as the
   panel â€” a mask that is dark-but-different is still a visible wedge, not a fixed icon.
   Left at the light value on a darkened panel they stop hiding anything and become the
   defect instead: a white disc with the uncovered ring geometry reading as black notches
   through it (BVY-340, reported on the notification-settings save confirmation).

   `!important` is load-bearing here in a way it is not in the v1 block. v1's white comes
   from the vendored `src/less/sweetalert.less`, so ordinary cascade order would beat it.
   swal2 instead assigns its mask colour as an INLINE style on each element at render
   time: `sweetalert2/dist/sweetalert2.js` selects exactly these three with
   `modal.querySelectorAll('[class^=swal2-success-circular-line], .swal2-success-fix')` and
   sets `params.background` on them, which defaults to `#fff`. An inline declaration
   outranks any author rule that is not `!important`, so drop it and this rule silently
   never applies.

   Covers every `type: 'success'` dialog in the legacy theme bundle, and is expected to
   cover the pageserver chapter-join flow too, which imports the same
   `theme/src/js/utils/sweetalert2` wrapper and injects its dialog into the body of the
   Django page that serves this sheet. The sibling icons need no equivalent: `error`
   paints its own `#f27474` x-mark and `warning`/`info`/`question` are bordered glyphs â€”
   the success masks are the only elements swal2 hands the panel colour to. */
[data-theme="dark"] .swal2-modal .swal2-success-circular-line-left:where(:not(.page-builder-content *, .rsvp-ticket-modal *)),
[data-theme="dark"] .swal2-modal .swal2-success-circular-line-right:where(:not(.page-builder-content *, .rsvp-ticket-modal *)),
[data-theme="dark"] .swal2-modal .swal2-success-fix:where(:not(.page-builder-content *, .rsvp-ticket-modal *)) {
  background-color: var(--theme-palette-light) !important;
}

/* sweetalert **v1** (`.sweet-alert`) â€” the same dialog family, a different library. Both
   are imported deliberately and coexist (see the note in
   redux/actions/ticketPurchaseWidget/tickets/index.js), so the swal2 block above never
   reached the v1 markup: the un-RSVP confirmation, the newsletter signup result and the
   discussions detail dialogs all render `.sweet-alert`, whose panel is a literal `white`
   in the vendored src/less/sweetalert.less, with its h2 at #333333 and its p at #797979 on
   top. Re-point the panel and both text roles at the neutral ramp, matching swal2.
   (DIS-3785)

   The success-icon rules below are NOT decoration. sweetalert v1 draws the green check by
   overlaying two rotated PANEL-COLOURED wedges (::before / ::after) and a `.sa-fix` bar on
   the circle; they are literally "paint the background over this part". Leaving them white
   on a darkened panel does not leave them invisible â€” it leaves three white slivers across
   the dialog. They must therefore resolve to the SAME token as the panel, not merely to
   something that contrasts with it. `type: 'success'` is live on public v1 surfaces
   (newslettersSignup.js, discussions/details.js), so this renders in practice.

   Not styled: the `show-input` variant (`.sweet-alert fieldset input`,
   `.sa-error-container`). No v1 call site in the repo opens an input dialog, so those
   rules never render and styling them would be unverifiable. */
[data-theme="dark"] .sweet-alert:where(:not(.page-builder-content *, .rsvp-ticket-modal *)) {
  background-color: var(--theme-palette-light) !important;
}
[data-theme="dark"] .sweet-alert h2:where(:not(.page-builder-content *, .rsvp-ticket-modal *)),
[data-theme="dark"] .sweet-alert p:where(:not(.page-builder-content *, .rsvp-ticket-modal *)) {
  color: var(--theme-palette-dark) !important;
}
[data-theme="dark"] .sweet-alert .sa-icon.sa-success:where(:not(.page-builder-content *, .rsvp-ticket-modal *))::before,
[data-theme="dark"] .sweet-alert .sa-icon.sa-success:where(:not(.page-builder-content *, .rsvp-ticket-modal *))::after,
[data-theme="dark"] .sweet-alert .sa-icon.sa-success .sa-fix:where(:not(.page-builder-content *, .rsvp-ticket-modal *)) {
  background-color: var(--theme-palette-light) !important;
}

/* Blog post titles (compiled brand gray, not admin-driven) */
[data-theme="dark"] .blog-posts .blog-live-title > a:where(:not(.page-builder-content *, .rsvp-ticket-modal *)),
[data-theme="dark"] .blog-live-title > a:where(:not(.page-builder-content *, .rsvp-ticket-modal *)) {
  color: var(--theme-palette-dark) !important;
}

/* Bootstrap dropdown panels â€” the user-menu dropdown and the per-item â‹® (kebab)
   menus (notifications, etc.) render a Bootstrap `.dropdown-menu` panel that is
   compiled-LESS white (Bootstrap default) and is not reached by the chrome/token
   overrides above, so it's white-on-white in dark (DM-023, DM-030). Re-point the
   panel, its items, hover state and dividers at the dark neutral ramp. */
[data-theme="dark"] .dropdown-menu:where(:not(.page-builder-content *, .rsvp-ticket-modal *)) {
  background-color: var(--theme-palette-light) !important;
  border-color: var(--theme-palette-light-borders) !important;
}
[data-theme="dark"] .dropdown-menu > li > a:where(:not(.page-builder-content *, .rsvp-ticket-modal *)),
[data-theme="dark"] .dropdown-menu > li > button:where(:not(.page-builder-content *, .rsvp-ticket-modal *)),
[data-theme="dark"] .dropdown-menu li > a:where(:not(.page-builder-content *, .rsvp-ticket-modal *)),
[data-theme="dark"] .dropdown-menu [class*="Text"]:where(:not(.page-builder-content *, .rsvp-ticket-modal *)) {
  color: var(--theme-palette-dark) !important;
}
[data-theme="dark"] .dropdown-menu > li > a:hover:where(:not(.page-builder-content *, .rsvp-ticket-modal *)),
[data-theme="dark"] .dropdown-menu > li > a:focus:where(:not(.page-builder-content *, .rsvp-ticket-modal *)),
[data-theme="dark"] .dropdown-menu > li > button:hover:where(:not(.page-builder-content *, .rsvp-ticket-modal *)),
[data-theme="dark"] .dropdown-menu > li > button:focus:where(:not(.page-builder-content *, .rsvp-ticket-modal *)) {
  background-color: var(--theme-palette-soft-light) !important;
  color: var(--theme-palette-dark) !important;
}
[data-theme="dark"] .dropdown-menu .divider:where(:not(.page-builder-content *, .rsvp-ticket-modal *)) {
  background-color: var(--theme-palette-light-borders) !important;
}

/* Notifications dropdown (BDS NotificationsMenu island â€” the bell popover, DIS-3585).
   The BDS component ships hard-coded LIGHT hex (panel #fff, chrome borders #d3dce4,
   unread/hover band #f6f9fb, message text #3c4153) and reads none of the --Color*
   tokens, so the whole popover renders as a white box with dark text over the dark
   UI. This block is a 1:1 mirror of next/src/app/dark-overrides.css (the /next bell
   dropdown uses the SAME BDS component) so the two match. The broad __unread match is
   intentional â€” it also re-points the unread DOT fill to the soft token, giving the
   same subtle ring /next shows instead of a hard red dot. Substring class matches
   survive the BDS build-hash suffixes (â€¦_aggpl / â€¦_47za0). */
[data-theme="dark"] [class*="NotificationsMenuItem-styles__unread"]:where(:not(.page-builder-content *, .rsvp-ticket-modal *)),
[data-theme="dark"] [class*="NotificationsMenuItem-styles__interactive"]:hover:where(:not(.page-builder-content *, .rsvp-ticket-modal *)),
[data-theme="dark"] [class*="NotificationsMenu-styles__viewAll"]:hover:where(:not(.page-builder-content *, .rsvp-ticket-modal *)) {
  background: var(--theme-palette-soft-light) !important;
}
[data-theme="dark"] [class*="NotificationsMenuItem-styles__body"]:where(:not(.page-builder-content *, .rsvp-ticket-modal *)) {
  color: var(--theme-palette-dark) !important;
}
[data-theme="dark"] [class*="NotificationsMenu-styles__menu"]:where(:not(.page-builder-content *, .rsvp-ticket-modal *)) {
  background: var(--theme-palette-light) !important;
  border-color: var(--theme-palette-light-borders) !important;
}
[data-theme="dark"] [class*="NotificationsMenu-styles__header_"]:where(:not(.page-builder-content *, .rsvp-ticket-modal *)) {
  border-bottom-color: var(--theme-palette-light-borders) !important;
}
[data-theme="dark"] [class*="NotificationsMenu-styles__footer"]:where(:not(.page-builder-content *, .rsvp-ticket-modal *)) {
